(project pricklypear-auth
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: auth")
 (status partial)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section auth
  (doc "Multi-user identity: credentials, sessions, login, and binding each human to their garden")
  (status partial)
  (* agent note (|2026-07-12 status-reconcile: Web login + admin + personal garden identity ship in-image. argon2, full multi-user process isolation, rich credential kinds still open.|) *)
  (* agent note (|2026-07-12: PRODUCT CORRECTION. Multi-user is the system,
    not an optional phase and not cancelled by the single-image deploy
    pivot. Single-image simplified process topology (one binary to ship);
    it does NOT mean single-human. Complete auth = many users, each with
    credentials + session + personal branch + authorship; process-per-user
    isolation remains the isolation model (multi-user.isolation) when the
    fleet is live. nginx basic-auth is a temporary gate, not the product.
    Spec below is the complete multi-user auth system for implementation.|) *)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Auth is its own unikernel, separate from the
        supervisor. This is deliberate, not just
        componentization:

        - AUDIT/TRUST BOUNDARY: auth holds credentials
          and verifies them. If compromised, the whole
          system is compromised. This image should be
          small, boring, never touched. The supervisor
          changes often; auth changes rarely. Separating
          means supervisor changes never risk auth code.
        - BLAST RADIUS: a memory bug in the supervisor
          cannot reach into auth's address space to read
          credentials. (Both are unikernels with their
          own address spaces; no shared memory.)
        - RESTART INDEPENDENCE: supervisor restarts
          (new fleet logic shipped) don't kill active
          sessions (auth keeps running, tokens stay
          valid). Auth restarts (rare) are explicit.
        - DEPENDENCY HYGIENE: auth pulls crypto
          primitives (argon2, ed25519, HMAC). Supervisor
          pulls process management. Each unikernel's
          dep surface stays small and Mirage-idiomatic.

        Auth's job is FOUR PURE FUNCTIONS of the
        credential store (in Postgres):
          verify(cred) -> May<user_id>
          lookup(user_id) -> May<User>
          mint(user_id) -> token
          verify_token(token) -> May<user_id>
        Plus boot/attach provisioning, which is the
        supervisor's job, NOT auth's. Auth knows
        nothing about the fleet.

        Auth is the ONLY thing that holds the session
        token signing key. The supervisor never sees
        raw credentials (passwords, keys) past the
        initial verify call — it only sees user_ids and
        signed tokens. Good separation.
   |))
  )
  (subsection credentials-store
   (doc "The users, credentials, sessions, auth_log tables in Postgres")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|All tables shipped in migrations/002_auth.sql: users,
     credentials, auth_sessions [renamed from sessions to avoid the
     name clash with the image's sessions table, see BORGE-1783389549
     note], auth_log, system_keys. The `pricklypear_auth` PG role is
     GRANTed SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE ONLY on auth tables (not the
     branches/function_defs/data_rows tables) — defense-in-depth role
     isolation as the spec describes. auth/lib/store.ml connects as
     `pricklypear_auth`. All id columns have DEFAULT
     gen_random_uuid() (it5 migration fix). auth_log accumulates
     ssh_key verify rows verified end-to-end through /tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py.

     HARDENING TODO carried forward (documented in spec text below):
     move the session_signing key from system_keys (on-disk in PG) to
     auth-image MEMORY ONLY (env var on boot). v1 keeps the key in
     system_keys for restart survival; the spec calls this an
     accepted personal/small-team risk now.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Tables (added by migrations, see migrations):

           users (id uuid PK, username text UNIQUE,
                  is_admin bool, created_at timestamptz)

           credentials (id uuid PK, user_id uuid FK,
                        kind text,        -- 'ssh_key'|'password'|'web_token'|'api_key'
                        public_data jsonb,  -- SSH key material for verification;
                                            -- null for password (hash is secret)
                        secret_data jsonb,   -- argon2 hash for password;
                                            -- reference for api_key
                        created_at timestamptz,
                        last_used_at timestamptz)

           sessions (id uuid PK,            -- the token's opaque id
                     user_id uuid FK,
                     token_hash text,      -- HMAC of token; lookup target
                     expires_at timestamptz,
                     created_at timestamptz,
                     revoked_at timestamptz NULL)

           auth_log (id uuid PK, user_id text NULL,
                     kind text, success bool,
                     source_ip text,
                     happened_at timestamptz)

           system_keys (id text PK,        -- 'session_signing'
                        key_data bytea,    -- the HMAC signing key, raw
                        created_at timestamptz)

         KEY STORAGE (
          in-Postgres, role-isolated):
         - The session signing key lives in
           system_keys, read on auth boot.
         - system_keys is in a dedicated PG role
           that ONLY the auth image connects as.
           A DB dump from any other role does not
           include it (defense-in-depth, free).
         - KNOWN RISK: a DB dump from auth's role,
           or an auth-image compromise, leaks the
           signing key -> all web sessions forgeable.
           Accepted for now (personal/small-team OS).
         - HARDENING GOAL (when this becomes stable &
           useful): move the signing key to
           auth-image MEMORY ONLY (env var on boot,
           never touches disk). Tokens survive auth
           restart only if the same key re-supplied.
           Invalidating all sessions on key rotation
           is acceptable then.
         - Other credentials (password hashes via
           argon2id, SSH public keys) in Postgres
           permanently; that's correct and stable
           (these are public verification material,
           not signing keys).
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection verification
   (doc "verify(cred) / lookup / mint / verify_token — the four pure functions")
   (implements auth/lib/verify.ml auth/lib/tokens.ml)
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|2026-07-12 status-reconcile: Password verify works (interim sha256$). SSH-key/authd path retired with auth package. argon2id still deferred (BORGE-1783831698).|) *)
   (* agent note (|Implementation it5/verified it6 onward for the
     SSH-key path; token path verified by auth/test/auth_test.ml (4
     alcotest cases for HMAC determinism, random-opaque_id 32B,
     round-trip, malformed-token rejection).

     IMPLEMENTED:
     - `verify_ssh_key(presented_key) -> May<user_id>` full path:
       auth/lib/verify.ml + auth/lib/store.ml SELECT the
       credential WHERE kind='ssh_key' AND public_data=jsonb;
       returns Ok uid or Denied. End-to-end verified: denied key →
       {"ok":false}; admin key → {"ok":true,"user_id":"<uuid>"}.
     - `mint(user_id)` + `verify_token(token)` via auth/lib/tokens.ml:
       HMAC-SHA256 with the system_keys signing key; opaque_id is
       32 random bytes; token format is base64(opaque_id || sig);
       verify recomputes HMAC + compares. Mirage_crypto_rng
       initialized via Mirage_crypto_rng_unix.initialize (module
       Fortuna) on authd boot.

     NOT YET IMPLEMENTED (deferred — v1.5 / requires an opam install):
     - `verify_password(username, password)`: STUB only — raises
       `Not_implemented` because argon2 is NOT on the poohstack opam
       switch. No web-login password verification. ACCEPTABLE for v1
       because the SSH-first model means the primary credential IS
       the SSH key; password verification is the future web-login
       path. v1.5 will install `argon2` opam package + wire a real
       argon2id verify.

     Section marked `partial` until at least verify_password lands
     (the four pure functions the spec enumerates include both
     ssh_key AND password cred kinds).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         SSH KEY VERIFICATION (for supervisor SSH front door):
         1. Supervisor does SSH handshake, receives
            the presented public key.
         2. Supervisor calls auth:
            verify_ssh_key(presented_key) -> May<user_id>
         3. Auth: parse key, SELECT FROM credentials
            WHERE kind='ssh_key' AND public_data = key.
            Return user_id or denied.
         4. Supervisor calls auth.lookup(user_id) ->
            User record (for is_admin, etc.).
         5. Auth logs to auth_log.

         PASSWORD VERIFICATION (for web login):
         1. User POSTs username+password to the
            supervisor's /login (or the auth image's
            own endpoint — TBD; either way).
         2. Auth: SELECT secret_data FROM credentials
            WHERE kind='password' AND user_id=
            (SELECT id FROM users WHERE username=$1).
            argon2-verify against the presented
            password. Return user_id or denied.
         3. Auth logs to auth_log.

         TOKEN MINTING (after web login):
         1. Auth: generate random token bytes (32B),
            HMAC-sign with the session_signing key
            from system_keys, opaque_id = random,
            signed = HMAC(opaque_id).
         2. Auth: INSERT INTO sessions (id=opaque_id,
            user_id, token_hash=signed, expires_at=...).
         3. Auth: return token = base64(opaque_id ||
            signed). Client stores as cookie.

         TOKEN VERIFICATION (on web requests):
         1. Client sends token cookie. Routing layer
            (supervisor or relayd-style proxy) calls
            auth.verify_token(token).
         2. Auth: split token, recompute HMAC,
            compare; if match, lookup sessions row,
            check not revoked and not expired. Return
            user_id or denied.
         3. Auth logs to auth_log.

         TOKEN FORMAT (minimal, NOT JWT):
         opaque_id (32B random) || HMAC(opaque_id)
         We control both ends; don't need JWT's
         cross-system interop claims. Hash stored in
         sessions table enables revocation.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection bootstrap-admin
   (doc "First-admin creation via env var on auth first boot")
   (implements auth/lib/admin_api.ml auth/lib/boot.ml)
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|Implementation it5/verified it6 onward. auth/lib/
     boot.ml checks: if users table is empty AND PP_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_KEY
     env var is set, INSERT into users (username='admin',
     is_admin=true) + INSERT into credentials (kind='ssh_key',
     public_data=the key, user_id=admin). Verified end-to-end:
     /tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py sends the SAME admin SSH key as authd was
     supplied via env var on first boot; the verify_ssh_key call
     returns the admin's user_id. (When users table is non-empty, the
     env var is ignored on subsequent boots — idempotent bootstrap.)
     Subsequent users are NOT part of this subsection's done bar —
     see auth.user-management (planned; admin_api still stubs).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Bootstrapping the first user, with no users yet:

         1. Operator sets env var on the auth unikernel
            at first boot:
              PRICKLYPEAR_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_KEY=<ssh-ed25519 AAAA...>
         2. Auth boot: if users table is empty AND the
            env var is set, INSERT into users
            (username='admin', is_admin=true) and into
            credentials (kind='ssh_key',
            public_data=the key, user_id=admin).
         3. Auth boot: warn loudly in logs that
            bootstrap was used, and recommend clearing
            the env var afterward.
         4. After first boot, the env var is ignored.

         Subsequent users: added by an admin via the
         supervisor's admin UI (which itself authenticates
         via auth) calling an auth primitive
         (create-user username, add-credential user_id
         ssh_key, etc.). These primitives require
         is_admin=true on the calling session.

         No interactive bootstrap flow needed; the env-var
         pattern is simple, standard, and audit-logged.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection audit-log
   (doc "Every verification logged to auth_log")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|Implementation it5: auth/lib/store.ml's `audit_log`
     INSERTs a row (user_id, kind, success, source_ip=NULL,
     happened_at default now()) per verify_ssh_key call. Verified:
     `/tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py` succession of one successful admin verify
     + one denied verify leave behind two auth_log rows
     (kind='ssh_key', success=t then f). Use cases from the spec text
     below — synthesized log for anomalies (cred misuse, brute-force,
     insider abuse) — are achievable with the existing log shape; a
     future stats page on the supervisor admin could surface these.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Every call to verify_ssh_key, password
         verification, mint, and verify_token writes a
         row to auth_log:
           (user_id NULL if unknown/failed,
            kind: 'ssh_key'|'password'|'mint'|'verify_token',
            success bool,
            source_ip text,
            happened_at timestamptz default now())

         WHY from day one (despite personal-OS scope):
         - Cheap to add (one INSERT per verify call).
         - Invaluable when something goes wrong
           (compromised key? malicious insider? audit
           compliance if ever needed?).
         - Painful to retrofit (verification call sites
           scattered; adding log calls everywhere later
           means finding every one).
         - Standard practice; non-controversial.

         The log is append-only in practice
         (no UPDATE/DELETE on auth_log). Retention
         policy deferred; for now, keep forever
         (cheap rows).
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection rpc-from-supervisor
   (doc "How supervisor calls auth: internal signed/mTLS RPC")
   (implements supervisor/lib/auth_client.ml)
   (status deprecated)
   (* agent note (|2026-07-12 status-reconcile: Supervisor+authd RPC retired by single-image pivot. Auth is in-process.|) *)
   (* agent note (|Implementation it6/verified it6 onward. supervisor/
     lib/auth_client.ml does an HTTP POST to authd's /verify_ssh_key
     endpoint (hand-rolled HTTP/1.1 over plain Lwt_unix socket; reads
     response bytes until `}` or EOF; parses JSON; returns Ok uid or
     Denied). Fail-closed: any transport error logs + returns Denied.
     End-to-end verified by /tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py through the dev.sh stack:
     python connects to supervisor SSH stub :17822 → supervisor calls
     authd → authd verifies → image spawned. The supervisor itself never
     holds raw credentials in memory past the initial in-flight RPC call.
     (See BORGE-1783389549 for the note that hand-rolled HTTP-over-socket
     was needed because Lwt_io channels triggered EBADF mixed with raw
     Lwt_unix.read/write on the same fd.)

     DEFERRED from spec text: the actual spec doctrinal text below
     describes a signed/mTLS RPC. v1 uses plain HTTP+JSON on the
     internal network (loopback in dev, the inter-unikernel fabric in
     prod). The internal network IS the trust boundary in v1: images,
     supervisor, and authd share an isolated fabric; spoofing the
     authd address requires being on the fabric.

     Section marked `partial` until (1) a real mTLS or signed-token RPC
     layer lands (a future hardening iteration that would let authd
     REJECT unauthenticated supervisor RPCs — currently the
     supervisor's identity is implicit via network position). Also
     deferred: token-result passing through the supervisor (v1 returns
     the user_id straight from the SSH verify RPC, no separate token
     minting on the SSH path because the SSH session IS the auth).TOKEN
     verification RPC IS implemented (authd /verify_token endpoint)
     for the future web-login path.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The supervisor calls auth over an internal RPC
         (HTTP+JSON, or a tighter binary protocol — HTTP
         is simpler and auth doesn't need throughput).
         On the internal hypervisor network.

         AuthENTICATION OF THE RPC ITSELF:
         - mTLS between supervisor and auth:
           both present certs signed by a shared CA
           (the CA is the hypervisor itself, or a
           generated root for the deployment).
         - OR simpler: a shared static secret in a
           header, both images load from env vars,
           rotated manually. Less secure but v1-OK
           for a trusted internal network.
         - Either way: auth refuses calls from anything
           that doesn't present the right credential.
           The supervisor is the only legitimate caller.

         Endpoints auth exposes (internal only):
           POST /verify_ssh_key   (key) -> {user_id or denied}
           POST /verify_password  (user, pw) -> {user_id or denied}
           POST /mint_token       (user_id) -> {token, expires_at}
           POST /verify_token     (token) -> {user_id or denied}
           GET  /lookup/:user_id             -> {user} (admin only)
           POST /admin/create_user (admin only)
           POST /admin/add_credential (admin only)

         The supervisor holds a long-lived client cert
         (or shared secret) for these calls. It is the
         only caller; auth refuses all others.

         v1 SHIPPED: plain HTTP+JSON on internal network,
         /verify_ssh_key and token paths live.
         DEFERRED: mTLS/shared-secret RPC auth of the
         caller; full admin endpoint wiring (see
         auth.user-management).
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection multi-user-product
   (doc "COMPLETE multi-user product: many humans, each with identity, session, garden, authorship")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|2026-07-12 status-reconcile: Session+branch multi-user works (many users, personal gardens, admin gates). Process isolation / fleet remains deferred; argon2 deferred. Not full complete-system isolation story.|) *)
   (* agent note (|2026-07-12: authoritative multi-user auth product.
     Replaces "single-tenant only" reading of single-image.auth.
     Single-image = one binary to deploy; multi-user = many people.|) *)
   (verify
    (smoke "two users login independently; each sees own personal pins")
    (smoke "Alice define does not create own pin on Bob's branch")
    (smoke "non-admin cannot merge to system/main")
    (smoke "agent install on Alice session writes alice/personal only")
    (smoke "logout revokes session; next request requires login"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ============================================================
         MULTI-USER IS THE SYSTEM
         ============================================================

         Pricklypear is a multi-user Lisp OS. Multiple humans share
         one deployment. Each human has:
           - a user row + credentials
           - a browser (and/or API) session
           - a personal branch garden: <username>/personal
           - authorship on their defines (function_defs.author)
           - isolation from other users' gardens and (when fleet
             is live) from other users' processes

         single-image.borg simplifies HOW WE SHIP THE BINARY
         (one process to build/restart). It does NOT mean one human.
         multi-user.isolation (per-user image process) remains the
         isolation architecture for the complete system.

         ============================================================
         PERSONAS
         ============================================================

         Operator/admin — first user; can create users, merge to
           system/main, manage credentials, revoke sessions.
         Human user — logs in, works on <user>/personal, inherits
           system/main defaults, cannot admin-merge to system.
         Agent (as user) — authenticated as a human or service
           account; installs Lisp on that identity's branch;
           branch-locked for the task duration.
         Anonymous — only public routes (/login, health as policy).

         ============================================================
         USER STORIES (complete set)
         ============================================================

         US-BOOTSTRAP — First admin
           Empty users table → bootstrap admin from env
           (PP_BOOTSTRAP_USER / PP_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD) OR one-time
           /setup form that creates admin then disables itself.
           is_admin=true. Site thereafter requires auth.

         US-LOGIN — Browser sign-in
           GET /login → username + password form.
           POST /login → verify → mint session → Set-Cookie
           pp_session (HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/).
           Redirect /. Bad credentials → generic error + auth_log.

         US-LAND — Land in my computer
           After login (or valid cookie):
             1. Resolve user_id, username, is_admin from session.
             2. Ensure branch <username>/personal exists as EMPTY
                child of system/main (inheritance, not full copy).
             3. Image runtime identity = that user.
             4. Checkout <username>/personal if not already.
             5. (whoami) / chrome meta / define author = username.
           Alice never receives Bob's personal as current branch.

         US-LOGOUT — Sign out
           POST /logout → revoke auth_sessions row → clear cookie
           → /login.

         US-SESSION — Stay signed in
           Every non-public request: load session by cookie →
           valid unexpired unrevoked → attach identity; else
           redirect /login (HTML) or 401 (API).

         US-INVITE — Admin creates user
           Admin UI/API: create_user(username, is_admin?),
           set password credential, optional temp password.
           New user can US-LOGIN and gets own personal garden.

         US-AGENT — Agent or laptop as a user
           Authenticate via same session cookie (cookie jar) OR
           API key credential (Authorization: Bearer …) bound to
           a user_id. install_function / define write that user's
           current branch. Agent branch lock forbids checkout/
           fork/merge mid-task. Author may be agent:<model> with
           provenance; capability still runs as the user.

         US-ISOLATION — Process isolation (complete multi-user)
           Logging in boots or attaches that user's image process
           (supervisor fleet; multi-user.isolation). Alice's wedge
           does not take Bob offline. Auth never sees fleet; fleet
           never sees raw passwords — only user_id from verify.

         US-COTENANT — Logical multi-user in one process (allowed
           implementation shape when fleet is not running):
           One image process serves many sessions; identity is
           per-request; personal branch is per-session; no cross-
           user write to another's personal. Weaker blast radius
           than process isolation but correct namespacing.
           Complete system MUST support US-ISOLATION; cotenant is
           not a substitute for the isolation model long-term.

         US-PERMS — Authorization
           - define/route/deftype → current branch only
           - merge to system/* → admin only
           - merge to own owner/* → free for that owner
           - admin user CRUD → admin session only
           - data_rows shared (existing rule) unless future
             row-level owner column is added

         US-AUDIT — Failures are boring and logged
           auth_log for login success/fail, logout, admin acts.
           No username enumeration in errors.

         ============================================================
         END-TO-END STORY
         ============================================================

         1. Deploy; bootstrap admin.
         2. Admin opens https://host/ → /login → signs in →
            admin/personal home.
         3. Admin creates user "alice" + password.
         4. Alice signs in → alice/personal (empty garden),
            inherits system/main tools.
         5. Alice edits ui/menu → pin on alice/personal only.
         6. Bob signs in → bob/personal; does not see Alice's pin
            as own; may inherit same main defaults.
         7. Agent with Alice session installs helper → alice/personal.
         8. Alice logout; cookie dead.
         9. With fleet: Alice's image process is hers; Bob's is his.

         ============================================================
         OBJECTS (complete)
         ============================================================

         users(id, username UNIQUE, is_admin, created_at)
         credentials(id, user_id, kind, public_data, secret_data,
                     created_at, last_used_at)
           kind: password | api_key | ssh_key | …
         auth_sessions(id, user_id, token_hash, expires_at,
                       created_at, revoked_at, user_agent, source_ip)
         auth_log(id, user_id NULL, kind, success, source_ip,
                  happened_at)

         Runtime image session (per connection / request):
           user_id, username, is_admin
           store.branch_id → <username>/personal (default)
           author for define = username | agent:<model>

         Password hashing: argon2id (complete system). Interim
         hash only acceptable until argon2 is available; must be
         upgraded in place.

         Session token: opaque random id; store only HMAC/hash in
         DB; cookie carries raw id. Revocable. Expiry required.

         ============================================================
         HTTP SURFACE (complete)
         ============================================================

         Public:
           GET  /login
           POST /login
           GET  /health   (policy: public or authed — choose public
                           for ops probes)

         Authenticated (any user):
           POST /logout
           all product routes (/, /todos, /notes, /api/eval, …)
           identity-dependent branch + author

         Admin:
           /admin/users  (list, create, set password, revoke sessions)
           merge policy to system/* already enforced in Lisp prims

         API:
           Cookie session OR Authorization: Bearer <api_key>
           same identity attachment as browser

         nginx: TLS termination; reverse proxy to image(s).
         Complete system does NOT rely on htpasswd as the user
         database. htpasswd may exist only as emergency break-glass.

         ============================================================
         FLEET ATTACH (complete multi-user isolation)
         ============================================================

         On successful login / SSH verify:
           supervisor (or attach layer) resolves user_id →
           Fleet.boot_or_attach(user) → image with env:
             PP_USER_ID, PP_USERNAME, PP_BRANCH_ID, PP_BRANCH_NAME
           Proxy HTTP/REPL to that instance.

         Auth component: verify + mint only. Never boots images.
         Image: trusts identity from supervisor/session attach;
         does not re-verify password every request if session token
         already verified at the edge of the image.

         ============================================================
         RELATION TO BRANCHES (already built)
         ============================================================

         system/main     — package defaults (seed from libs/*.pp)
         <user>/personal — empty-child garden; pins are "own"
         Effective Functions list = deduped closest-wins
         Agent locked to current branch during agent-task

         Auth does not replace branching; it answers WHO is
         on which garden.

         ============================================================
         NON-GOALS
         ============================================================

         - OAuth / OIDC / social login
         - Email magic links (unless added later as a credential kind)
         - Sharing one password (htpasswd) among humans as the
           permanent multi-user model
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection login-experience
   (doc "What log in means: I present credentials and land in MY live image")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-07-12 status-reconcile: Login binds identity, ensure_personal + checkout personal, whoami is session user. Fleet attach deferred (single-image).|) *)
   (verify
    (smoke "human authenticates and lands on username/personal")
    (smoke "unknown credential denied; no image attach for stranger")
    (smoke "two concurrent users keep distinct identities"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         Product bar (from multi-user-product):
         1. Present credential (password browser, API key,
            or SSH key when SSH front door is live).
         2. Verify → user_id (never proceed on failure).
         3. Mint or resume session; attach identity.
         4. Ensure <username>/personal; set runtime branch.
         5. With fleet: boot/attach that user's image.
         6. User works; logout/revoke ends session.

         Success: "I logged into my computer as me."
         Failure modes never leak other users' existence
         beyond generic invalid-credentials.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection web-login
   (doc "Browser password login, session cookie, middleware, logout")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-07-12 status-reconcile: GET/POST /login, pp_session cookie, middleware, logout ship in image/lib/http.ml + auth_store.ml. Interim password hash sha256$ (BORGE-1783831698); argon2 still deferred.|) *)
   (implements image/lib/http.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build")
    (smoke "POST /login valid -> Set-Cookie -> GET / as that user")
    (smoke "POST /login invalid -> 401 + auth_log fail")
    (smoke "POST /logout revokes; subsequent GET / redirects login")
    (smoke "Alice and Bob cookies do not cross personal branches"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         Complete browser path (part of multi-user-product):

         GET  /login
           Public form: username, password. No product chrome
           that assumes identity.

         POST /login
           Body: username, password (+ CSRF token when forms
           are cookie-authenticated for other posts).
           verify_password against credentials.kind=password.
           On success: insert auth_sessions; Set-Cookie
           pp_session=<opaque>; redirect /.
           On failure: auth_log; same error for bad user/pass.

         Middleware (all non-public routes)
           Read cookie → hash → lookup auth_sessions →
           check expires_at / revoked_at → load users row →
           bind Session identity (user_id, username, is_admin) →
           ensure_personal + checkout if needed.

         POST /logout
           Revoke session; clear cookie; redirect /login.

         Password: argon2id verify/hash in complete system.
         Cookie flags: HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/.

         nginx: TLS + reverse_proxy only for this path.
         htpasswd is not the user database.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection user-management
   (doc "Admin creates and manages users and credentials")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-07-12 status-reconcile: /admin/users create user, set password, revoke sessions; bootstrap_admin_if_empty. api_key/ssh_key credential UX still thin.|) *)
   (* agent note (|admin_api create/list paths existed under authd;
     complete system needs in-product /admin/users UI + password
     credentials + revoke session, wired to the same users table.|) *)
   (verify
    (smoke "admin creates user + password; new user logs in on own personal")
    (smoke "non-admin cannot create users")
    (smoke "admin can revoke all sessions for a user"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         Complete admin surface:
           create_user(username, is_admin?)
           set_password(user_id, password)
           add_credential(user_id, kind, …)  ; api_key, ssh_key
           list_users / get_user
           revoke_sessions(user_id)
           revoke_credential(id)

         First admin: bootstrap-admin (env) when users empty.
         All later humans: this API/UI only.

         UI: /admin/users (admin session required).
         Enforcement: is_admin on session; 403 otherwise.

         Credentials order of support in complete system:
           password (web-login), api_key (agents/pp_eval),
           ssh_key (SSH front door when live).
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection identity-and-branch
   (doc "Session identity drives personal garden and authorship")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-07-12 status-reconcile: ensure_personal_branch + checkout_personal at session make; define authorship uses username; agent_branch_lock exists.|) *)
   (verify
    (smoke "whoami equals session username after login")
    (smoke "define lands on username/personal")
    (smoke "Functions origin own only for true personal pins"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         On every authenticated request / image attach:

           ensure_branch owner=username name=personal
             parent=system/main  (EMPTY child; CTE inherit)
           store.branch_id = that id
           manifest.username = username
           manifest.branch_name = username/personal

         (define name body) persists with author=username
         (or agent:<model> when agent installs under policy).

         Functions UI: effective one-name-one-row; origin own
         vs inherited (already implemented).

         Agent-task: agent_branch_lock blocks checkout/fork/merge
         (already implemented); must run under the user's session.

         Changing user mid-process (cotenant): rebind identity +
         checkout personal for that user; never leave previous
         user's pins writable.
    |))
   )
  )
 )
)
